WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.650
The payments program has just authorized a payment, pay-3: the bank has set the money aside.

2
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.207
It saves the payment's row in its database, PostgreSQL.

3
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:17.615
Now the ledger, the program that books every movement of money, needs to hear about it.

4
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.054
The message is an event called PaymentAuthorized.

5
00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:27.748
It travels through Kafka, a program that keeps streams of messages in order, for other programs to read.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.212
So how do we get the event out exactly when the row is saved, and never when it isn't?

7
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.800
The obvious way: save the row, then send the event.

8
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.905
But that's a dual write, two writes to two systems with nothing tying them together.

9
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:46.824
Crash between the lines, and the payment is saved, but the ledger never hears.

10
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.033
Swap the lines, and it's worse.

11
00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:54.644
Send first, then crash before the save, and the ledger books a payment that doesn't exist.

12
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.384
So one order loses news, and the other spreads rumours.

13
00:00:58.384 --> 00:01:00.348
Neither is what a ledger wants.

14
00:01:00.348 --> 00:01:03.389
Two systems, and no all or nothing between them.

15
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:05.868
Second try: don't send at all.

16
00:01:05.868 --> 00:01:14.051
Let Debezium, a program that reads a database's log and turns each change into a message, follow the payment table.

17
00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:15.784
No dual write now.

18
00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:20.787
But every reader gets your table's columns, so renaming one breaks them.

19
00:01:20.787 --> 00:01:23.984
And the message says only what the row became.

20
00:01:23.984 --> 00:01:24.609
Captured?

21
00:01:24.609 --> 00:01:26.555
Or someone fixing a mistake?

22
00:01:26.555 --> 00:01:27.736
It can't say why.

23
00:01:27.967 --> 00:01:34.160
What we want is an event we designed, saved together with the row, so both happen or neither does.

24
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:37.044
Think of an old carbon-copy form at a shop.

25
00:01:37.044 --> 00:01:43.933
You write the sale on the top sheet, and the same stroke of the pen writes the courier's note on the sheet underneath.

26
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:46.072
One can't exist without the other.

27
00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:50.388
And the courier takes only the bottom sheet, the one written for the courier.

28
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:51.102
Map it.

29
00:01:51.102 --> 00:01:53.310
The top sheet is the payment row.

30
00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:58.931
The bottom sheet is a second row, in a table called outbox, written for the readers.

31
00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:06.265
The one stroke is a single database transaction, a group of changes saved all together, or not at all.

32
00:02:06.265 --> 00:02:09.953
And the courier is Debezium, reading only the outbox.

33
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.574
Watch the log.

34
00:02:11.574 --> 00:02:13.831
BEGIN, transaction 771.

35
00:02:13.831 --> 00:02:16.088
Insert the payment row.

36
00:02:16.088 --> 00:02:21.386
Insert the outbox row, event e-66 for account acct-17.

37
00:02:21.386 --> 00:02:22.073
COMMIT.

38
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:27.713
Debezium reads the committed transaction and picks up only the outbox insert.

39
00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:32.610
Now a second payment, pay-4, with its outbox row e-77.

40
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:37.717
The program crashes before commit, so the database rolls it back.

41
00:02:37.717 --> 00:02:39.445
Does e-77 reach Kafka?

42
00:02:39.445 --> 00:02:40.703
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:44.102
No.

44
00:02:44.102 --> 00:02:49.692
Its rows were in the log, but the log reader hands out only committed transactions.

45
00:02:49.692 --> 00:02:53.733
No event without a payment, and no payment without an event.

46
00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.943
This is called the transactional outbox.

47
00:02:56.943 --> 00:03:01.760
Debezium's outbox router turns each outbox row into a Kafka record.

48
00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:05.174
The topic comes from the row's kind, payment.

49
00:03:05.174 --> 00:03:10.200
The key is the account, acct-17, so each account's events stay in order.

50
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.829
And the event's id, e-66, travels along in a header.

51
00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:18.565
The program can even delete the outbox row in the same transaction.

52
00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:22.123
The router ignores deletes, so the table stays empty.

53
00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:26.609
Why not a small program that checks the outbox table every second?

54
00:03:26.609 --> 00:03:32.009
It costs a query every second, and it can skip a row whose transaction commits late.

55
00:03:32.009 --> 00:03:36.509
The log hands out transactions in commit order, so nothing slips past.

56
00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:39.219
One thing the outbox doesn't fix.

57
00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:43.753
Debezium saves its place in the log only about once a minute.

58
00:03:43.753 --> 00:03:48.435
Say e-88 reaches Kafka, and Debezium dies before the next save.

59
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:54.723
After a restart, it reads from the old place and sends e-88 again, with the same id.

60
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.235
So the ledger keeps the ids it has applied, and drops repeats.

61
00:03:59.235 --> 00:04:02.992
Delivery is at least once, never exactly once for free.

62
00:04:03.233 --> 00:04:08.275
In Java and Kotlin, it's one transaction with two inserts, and one commit.

63
00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:14.740
Publish what you meant, not what your table looks like, and write it in the same transaction.

64
00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:16.317
One quick check.

65
00:04:16.317 --> 00:04:21.466
A payment and its outbox row commit, and the program crashes a moment later.

66
00:04:21.466 --> 00:04:23.499
Does the ledger hear about it?

67
00:04:23.499 --> 00:04:24.583
Pause and think.

68
00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:28.063
Yes.

69
00:04:28.063 --> 00:04:34.351
The commit is in the log, and Debezium reads it, whether the program is alive or not.
